<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <artifactId>forge</artifactId>
        <groupId>forge</groupId>
        <version>${revision}</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>forge-gui-teavm</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <name>Forge TeaVM Web</name>

    <!--
      TeaVM / gdx-teavm viability spec module.

      This module targets an HTML5/WebGL deployment of the Forge mobile UI using
      the gdx-teavm backend (https://github.com/xpenatan/gdx-teavm).  It is the
      recommended successor to the forge-gui-html GWT module because:

        1. No source JARs required – TeaVM compiles from compiled .class files
           (bytecode), not Java source code.  This removes the biggest obstacle
           in the GWT approach, which required source jars for every transitive
           dependency.
        2. LibGDX version alignment – gdx-teavm 1.5.x targets LibGDX 1.14.x.
           Only a minor LibGDX version bump (1.13.5 → 1.14.0) is needed.
        3. Better Java API coverage – TeaVM emulates a wider subset of the Java
           standard library than GWT, reducing the need for stub replacements.
        4. Active maintenance – gdx-backend-gwt was effectively abandoned after
           LibGDX 1.9.14 (2019); gdx-teavm is actively developed.

      Build architecture
      ==================
      The module contains two distinct entry points:

        * BuildForgeTeaVM.java  – a build-time Java program that drives the
          TeaCompiler.  It is invoked by exec-maven-plugin and produces the
          deployable JS / Wasm output.

        * TeaVMLauncher.java    – the browser-side entry point that is compiled
          INTO JavaScript by the TeaCompiler.  It instantiates WebApplication
          and passes in the Forge ApplicationListener.

      Known limitations / open work items
      =====================================
        1. LibGDX version bump
           gdx-teavm 1.5.x requires LibGDX 1.14.x.  forge-gui-mobile currently
           pins to 1.13.5.  Before this module can produce a working build the
           gdx version in forge-gui-mobile/pom.xml (and the other platform POMs)
           must be bumped to 1.14.0 (or whatever version gdx-teavm targets).

        2. IDeviceAdapter – incompatible imports
           org.jupnp.UpnpServiceConfiguration is referenced in the interface
           signature of IDeviceAdapter.getUpnpPlatformService().  org.jupnp is
           not TeaVM-compatible.  Options:
             a) Extract the UPnP method into a sub-interface (IUpnpProvider) and
                have IDeviceAdapter extend it only on non-web platforms.
             b) Change the return type to Object and cast at the call site.
             c) Guard the call site with a platform check.
           See TeaVMAdapter.java for the current stub approach.

        3. IDeviceAdapter – java.io.File in default method
           IDeviceAdapter.isSupportedAudioFormat(File) uses java.io.File.  In
           TeaVM JS mode there is no filesystem, so File is unavailable.  The
           method signature needs to change to accept a String path instead of a
           File object, or the method must be moved out of the interface.

        4. forge/Forge.java – Sentry and java.nio.file
           Forge.java imports io.sentry.* and uses java.nio.file.Files /
           java.nio.file.Paths at startup.  Both are unavailable in TeaVM.
           The Sentry integration should be guarded by a platform check (e.g., a
           ForgeConstants.IS_WEB flag) and the file-system checks should be
           replaced with LibGDX FileHandle equivalents.

        5. AudioClip.java – java.io.File and Thread.sleep
           forge/sound/AudioClip.java uses java.io.File and Thread.sleep.
           Audio paths should be passed as strings (usable with
           Gdx.files.internal()) and the Thread.sleep call removed.

        6. LibGDXImageFetcher.java – java.net.HttpURLConnection, java.nio.file
           Uses Java SE HTTP and NIO file APIs.  Replace with LibGDX's
           com.badlogic.gdx.Net.HttpRequest for image downloads, and use
           Gdx.files for writes.

        7. Box2D native library
           forge-gui-mobile depends on gdx-box2d (the adventure mode uses it).
           gdx-teavm currently does not ship a pre-compiled Box2D Wasm module.
           Adventure mode will be unavailable on the web until box2d emscripten
           support is added to gdx-teavm or adventure mode is disabled at
           compile-time for the web target.

        8. Asset bundling
           The TeaCompiler copies assets from a directory specified in
           BuildForgeTeaVM.java into the output webapp.  For a real build the
           assets directory must contain all game resources (res/, skins/, etc.).
           A CDN/download strategy is likely needed given the multi-GB asset set.

        9. Audio
           gdx-teavm uses Howler.js for audio.  Only .mp3 and .ogg are widely
           supported.  The .midi files used for background music will not work
           natively – either convert to .ogg or disable music on the web target.

       10. Reflection / Class.forName
           Several Forge screens (NewGameMenu, LoadGameMenu, OnlineMenu) use
           reflection.  TeaVM has limited reflection support; these classes may
           need to be refactored to avoid runtime class lookup.

       11. Threading (FThreads)
           Forge uses FThreads / ThreadUtil for long-running game operations.
           TeaVM's JS target is single-threaded.  The WASM target (future) may
           support threads.  For the JS build, blocking calls in game logic must
           be converted to async/callback patterns or run inside Web Workers.

      How to build (once prerequisites are met)
      ==========================================
        mvn -pl forge-gui-teavm -Pteavm package

      The output is placed in target/dist/webapp/ and can be served from any
      static HTTP server.  An embedded Jetty server is started automatically
      when BuildForgeTeaVM.java sets startJettyAfterBuild = true.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!--
          gdx-teavm 1.5.3 targets LibGDX 1.14.0.
          NOTE: forge-gui-mobile currently uses 1.13.5.  Before this profile
          can produce a runnable build, the gdx version across all modules must
          be bumped to 1.14.0.
        -->
        <gdx.teavm.version>1.5.3</gdx.teavm.version>
        <!--
          The gdx version used by this module must match what gdx-teavm expects.
          Keep in sync with forge-gui-mobile/pom.xml once that module is bumped.
        -->
        <gdx.version>1.14.0</gdx.version>
    </properties>

    <repositories>
        <!--
          gdx-teavm stable releases are published to Maven Central (the default
          Maven repository) so no additional repository declaration is needed
          for production builds using a tagged release.

          To use a -SNAPSHOT build of gdx-teavm, add the Central Portal snapshot
          repository as documented at https://github.com/xpenatan/gdx-teavm:
            <repository>
              <id>sonatype-snapshots</id>
              <url>https://central.sonatype.com/repository/maven-snapshots/</url>
              <releases><enabled>false</enabled></releases>
              <snapshots><enabled>true</enabled></snapshots>
            </repository>
        -->
    </repositories>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Forge mobile UI (the shared libgdx-based front-end) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>forge</groupId>
            <artifactId>forge-gui-mobile</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- LibGDX core (must match the version expected by gdx-teavm) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.badlogicgames.gdx</groupId>
            <artifactId>gdx</artifactId>
            <version>${gdx.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          gdx-teavm web backend.
          Contains:
            - WebApplication / WebApplicationConfiguration (runtime, compiled to JS)
            - TeaCompiler / WebBackend (build-time, runs on the JVM to produce JS)
            - Pre-built gdx.wasm.js (LibGDX native code compiled via Emscripten)
            - Howler.js (audio)
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.xpenatan.gdx-teavm</groupId>
            <artifactId>backend-web</artifactId>
            <version>${gdx.teavm.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          FreeType font rendering for TeaVM.
          Forge uses gdx-freetype for FSkinFont; this extension provides the
          Emscripten-compiled FreeType Wasm module for the browser.
          NOTE: the artifact id uses a hyphen (-), not a dot.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.xpenatan.gdx-teavm</groupId>
            <artifactId>gdx-freetype-teavm</artifactId>
            <version>${gdx.teavm.version}</version>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <!--
                      Both the build-time driver (BuildForgeTeaVM) and the
                      browser runtime entry point (TeaVMLauncher) live in the
                      same source tree and are compiled by javac.  The TeaCompiler
                      then cross-compiles TeaVMLauncher (and its dependencies) from
                      the resulting bytecode into JavaScript.
                    -->
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <!--
      Activate this profile with -Pteavm to compile the TeaVM JS output:
        mvn -pl forge-gui-teavm -Pteavm package

      Prerequisites:
        - forge-gui-mobile and its transitive dependencies must already be built
          (run `mvn install` from the project root first).
        - The LibGDX version must be bumped to 1.14.0 (see notes above).
        - The API changes listed in the Known Limitations section above must be
          resolved before the TeaCompiler step will succeed.
    -->
    <profiles>
        <profile>
            <id>teavm</id>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <!--
                      Run the build-time TeaCompiler driver (BuildForgeTeaVM)
                      during the package phase.  The driver invokes the TeaCompiler
                      which transpiles TeaVMLauncher (and all reachable classes)
                      from JVM bytecode into JavaScript or WebAssembly.

                      Output: target/dist/webapp/
                              ├── index.html
                              ├── forge.js           (or forge.wasm)
                              ├── gdx.wasm.js        (LibGDX native Wasm)
                              ├── howler.js          (audio)
                              └── assets/            (game resources)
                    -->
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                        <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                        <version>3.3.0</version>
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <id>build-teavm</id>
                                <phase>package</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>java</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <mainClass>forge.teavm.BuildForgeTeaVM</mainClass>
                                    <!--
                                      The TeaCompiler consumes compiled class files, so the
                                      full compile-scope classpath (which includes all Forge
                                      modules compiled as JARs) must be on the classpath.
                                      exec:java uses the project's runtime classpath by default.
                                    -->
                                    <includeProjectDependencies>true</includeProjectDependencies>
                                    <includePluginDependencies>false</includePluginDependencies>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>
</project>
